    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.sessions\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nI use this configuration:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n      local sessions = require('mini.sessions') -- Session management (read, write, delete)\r\n      sessions.setup({\r\n        -- Whether to read latest session if Neovim opened without file arguments\r\n        autoread = true,\r\n\r\n        -- Whether to write current session before quitting Neovim\r\n        autowrite = true,\r\n\r\n        -- File for local session (use `''` to disable)\r\n        file = 'Session.vim',\r\n\r\n        -- Directory where global sessions are stored (use `''` to disable)\r\n        directory = '',\r\n\r\n        -- Whether to print session path after action\r\n        verbose = { read = true, write = true, delete = true },\r\n      })\r\n```\r\n\r\nand usually i create a session file everywhere i need one *or* i start neovim with `nvim .` or `nvim path/to/file.ext` so no autoloading a session happens and no error message appears.\r\n\r\nWhen i start `nvim` and have no directory-local `Session.vim`, i receive a rather obtrusive error:\r\n\r\n```\r\n12:40:12 msg_show Error executing lua callback: ...i/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.nvim/lua/mini/sessions.lua:587: (mini.sessions) There is no detected sessions. Change configuration and rerun `MiniSessions.setup()`.\r\nstack traceback:\r\n\t[C]: in function 'error'\r\n\t...i/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.nvim/lua/mini/sessions.lua:587: in function 'error'\r\n\t...i/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.nvim/lua/mini/sessions.lua:152: in function 'read'\r\n\t...i/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.nvim/lua/mini/sessions.lua:444: in function <...i/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini.nvim/lua/mini/sessions.lua:443>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there a straightforward way to either surpress this message completely (maybe not a good idea), make it \"nicer\" (e.g. removing the tracebacks) in `mini.sessions` configuration? Or is there a configuration which could help suppress this or make it less \"noisy\"?\r\n\r\nI understand this is maybe a (very) minor issue.",
